atial vision, very good leadership, sometimes not good at taking criticism, can be unorganized, not open to new ideas, show-off attitude. Candidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, concentrates very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, arrogant, weak leadership.\n\nWe need to decide recommendation. We need to consider stress resilience: we have no data for C, but we have data for A (calm in crisis) and B (handles stress well). So we might lean towards A or B. Also consider reliability: A is 100% reliable, B is very conscientious. Communication: A difficulty communicating, egocentric; B can be grumpy, uncooperative, makes nasty remarks. So both have interpersonal concerns. A creates positive atmosphere with crew, which is a plus. B's interpersonal issues seem more severe (nasty remarks). So A may be better overall despite communication difficulty? A's difficulty communicating ideas may affect crew cooperation, but positive atmosphere suggests some cooperation. Also A not willing to further education (adaptability). B's stress handling is good, but uncooperative.\n\nC has strong technical and leadership but unknown stress. D has responsibility but arrogant.\n\nThus likely A is best overall given known stress resilience and reliability, plus positive crew atmosphere, despite communication issues. We can argue that A's known calmness under pressure addresses the critical gap, and his reliability and positive crew atmosphere outweigh his egocentric tendency, which can be mitigated with training. So we vote Candidate A.\n\nWe need to reference prior discussion: agree with Agent 3 that stress data missing for C, and note that A has explicit calmness and reliability. Use facts: A quick correct decisions, calm in crisis, 100% reliable, creates positive atmosphere. Also note concerns: egocentric, difficulty communicating, not willing to further education. Compare to B's issues.\n\nThus produce public message: state recommendation f